"""Human-written ordinary prose; no record template or modulo-based construction."""
PASSAGES=[
('garden','''A gardener preparing a small bed first looks at the soil rather than immediately adding more water. The surface can look dry while a cooler layer underneath still contains plenty of moisture. She pushes a narrow trowel into one corner and lifts a small sample onto the path. Loose sandy soil falls apart readily, while soil containing more clay tends to hold together. Neither appearance alone tells her everything about what a particular plant needs. She checks several places because shade, roots and the slope of the bed can create local differences.
Along the fence, a young climbing plant has begun to reach toward a wooden support. Its newest stem is flexible and can be guided gently without forcing a sharp bend. The gardener leaves room inside a loose tie so the stem can grow thicker. A label near the roots records when the seed was planted. The date helps her compare its growth with another plant in a sunnier part of the garden. She notices that the two plants have produced different numbers of leaves, but she does not assume that sunlight is the only cause. Water, soil and the condition of the original seeds may also matter.
She carries a small watering can along the edge of the bed and pours slowly near the roots. Some water disappears quickly into the ground, while another patch takes longer to absorb it. Pausing between pours prevents a shallow stream from carrying soil onto the path. A few dry leaves have collected around a stone, so she moves them into a thin layer over an exposed patch. This cover can reduce how quickly the surface loses moisture. She keeps it away from the stems so their condition remains easy to inspect.
Another gardener arrives with a notebook showing observations from the previous week. They compare the appearance of the same bed on several mornings. One drawing shows where the fence casts its shadow, while another marks a patch that remained wet after rain. They walk around the bed together, checking those places against the notes. A narrow gap between two boards lets sunlight reach a small group of leaves. The position of that bright strip changes as the sun moves. The gardener makes a fresh sketch beside the earlier one and kneels to examine the next plant.'''),
('clouds','''Warm sunlight can supply energy that allows liquid water to evaporate from a lake, a puddle or damp soil. The water enters the air as vapor, which is not the same thing as the visible mist above a cold surface. Air can carry water vapor from one place to another. When a parcel of moist air rises, the pressure around it becomes lower and the parcel expands. Expansion is commonly associated with cooling. If the air becomes sufficiently cool, some vapor can condense onto tiny particles suspended in it.
The resulting droplets are small enough to remain carried by moving air. A cloud consists of many such droplets or, in colder conditions, ice particles. Its appearance depends on the amount of water present and on the way light passes through and around the particles. A thin cloud may look bright against a blue sky, while a thick layer can reduce the sunlight reaching the ground. Looking at the cloud from below gives a different view from looking at its sunlit side. A dark base does not mean that the water itself has changed color.
Inside a cloud, droplets can collide and combine. Other processes involving ice can also help produce particles large enough to fall. Whether falling water reaches the ground as rain, snow or another form depends on the conditions along its route. A snowflake can pass through warmer air and melt before reaching a street. A falling drop can also lose water through evaporation. The weather at the surface therefore reflects more than the temperature at a single height. Observations made at several levels help explain what is happening.
On a quiet afternoon, someone watching the sky might notice small clouds growing above a sunlit field. Their lower edges can remain at roughly similar heights while their tops change shape. Warm air rising from different parts of the landscape helps supply them with moisture. Wind can carry the clouds away from the places where that air first rose. A nearby hillside may influence the movement, especially when air is pushed upward along its slope. The observer compares the shape of one cloud with the next and watches a shadow move across the field below.'''),
('station_story','''Nora arrived at the small station before the ticket office opened. A pale band of sunlight reached across the platform, touching the edge of a wooden bench. She set her bag beside it and looked at the notice attached to the wall. The departure time matched the one she had written on a folded piece of paper. Across the tracks, a worker was sweeping leaves away from a drain. The sound of the brush carried clearly through the quiet air.
A man with a bicycle stopped near the entrance and studied the same notice. He asked Nora whether the next train allowed bicycles. She pointed to a small symbol beneath the timetable but suggested checking with the station worker when he came closer. The man leaned his bicycle against a railing and took a bottle of water from his bag. They spoke about the path leading from the station to the river. Nora had walked part of it the previous summer and remembered a narrow bridge beside a mill.
The ticket office window opened with a short scrape. A woman inside placed a stack of leaflets on the counter and turned the sign toward the platform. Nora bought her ticket and asked which end of the train would stop nearest the exit at her destination. The woman described the platform there and showed her a small printed map. Nora folded the map along an existing crease and put it beside the ticket. The man with the bicycle approached the window after her.
While they waited, a child and her grandfather began counting the painted posts along the fence. The child lost her place when a bird landed on the nearest post and tried again from the beginning. Her grandfather held a small paper bag that smelled faintly of bread. A soft announcement came from the speaker above the office. People moved a little farther from the edge of the platform and looked toward the bend in the tracks. Nora could hear a train before she could see it. She lifted her bag from the bench and watched the station worker point toward the approaching carriages.'''),
('bicycle','''Before taking a bicycle out, a rider can learn a great deal by looking at it carefully and moving it slowly by hand. The tires should have enough air for their type and the expected load. Pressing a tire gives only a rough impression, so a suitable gauge can be useful. The rider checks the recommended range marked on the tire rather than assuming every bicycle uses the same pressure. A tire that has lost air may also need examination for a damaged valve or a small puncture.
Spinning each wheel slowly can reveal whether it rubs against a brake or another part of the frame. The rider listens for an unusual sound and watches the gap on either side of the rim. A leaf caught near a wheel is different from a loose component, even if both make noise. The wheel should be held securely in place. If the rider is unfamiliar with its fastening system, the manufacturer's instructions or help from a knowledgeable mechanic are more useful than guessing. The bicycle remains supported while these checks are made.
The brakes can be tested while walking the bicycle forward. Applying one lever at a time shows whether the corresponding wheel slows as expected. The lever should not require an unusual amount of travel before the brake begins to act. Looking at the cables or hoses may reveal visible damage, but not every problem can be diagnosed from appearance alone. The rider also checks that the handlebars and saddle are secure. A small movement during a stationary check can indicate a problem worth resolving before riding.
The chain and gears need attention as well. Heavy dirt can interfere with movement, while obvious damage may require repair rather than simple cleaning. Turning the pedals by hand with the bicycle appropriately supported lets the rider observe how the chain passes through the mechanism. Clothing and fingers are kept clear of moving parts. Lights and reflectors are checked if they will be needed on the planned route. The rider then looks over the path and weather information, considering which equipment and clothing are suitable for that particular journey.'''),
('pottery','''The potter places a newly shaped bowl on a wooden board beside the wheel. Its surface is still damp enough to reflect light from the window. She turns the board slowly and studies the curve of the rim from several angles. One shallow mark shows where a finger pressed more firmly into the clay. A damp sponge can soften the mark, but too much water would make the surface difficult to handle. She touches the sponge to a spare piece of clay before returning to the bowl.
On a neighboring shelf, another piece has become firmer. It can be lifted carefully, although its base still needs attention. The potter compares the thickness of its rim with the rim of the newer bowl. Small differences in moisture change how readily the clay bends under pressure. She makes a note about the time each piece has spent on the shelf. Air movement through the room can make one side dry faster than another. Turning a board occasionally helps her observe whether that difference is becoming important.
A narrow wooden tool lies beside a folded cloth. Its rounded edge can reach part of the inside curve without leaving the same marks as a sharper tool. The potter holds it loosely at first and checks the angle against an unused lump of clay. The marks left by the trial show which direction gives a smoother result. She changes her grip and repeats the movement. Each attempt removes only a small amount of material. Keeping the original curve in mind matters more than making a large correction quickly.
A second worker brings a shallow tray containing tools of several sizes. They arrange the tools so that their working edges do not strike one another. The worker points out a small irregularity near the bottom of the firmer bowl. Together they look at the piece in the light before deciding how to approach it. The potter supports the bowl with one hand while examining the base with the other. A little clay has collected along one edge, and the wooden board shows a faint damp ring beneath it. She places the piece back on the cloth and selects a smaller tool from the tray.'''),
('library','''A librarian places a returned book on a sorting table and checks the number inside its cover. The number matches an entry in a borrowing record, where a pencil mark shows when the book left the shelf. She adds the return date and moves the book to a tray for inspection. Most of its pages are in good condition, but one corner of the cover has begun to bend. A strip of protective material lies beside the tray. Before using it, she checks whether it would interfere with the way the cover opens.
Another worker brings several books from the reading room and puts them in a separate stack. These have remained inside the building and do not require return entries. Their shelf labels nevertheless need checking because two categories stand close together. A guide to local birds belongs near the nature books, while a collection of stories belongs elsewhere. The workers compare the labels with a simple list of subjects. They leave space between the stacks so that books awaiting repair will not be mixed with books ready to shelve.
A visitor approaches the desk with a question about maps. The librarian points toward a low cabinet and explains how the drawers are labeled. Some contain maps of the town, while others cover the surrounding hills. The visitor is interested in a footpath beside the river and wants to compare two routes. The librarian brings a clear protective sheet to put over the larger map while they look at it. This keeps pencils and other objects from marking its surface. They trace the path with their fingers rather than drawing directly on the paper.
At the sorting table, the other worker finds a label that has faded. He puts the book beside a fresh sheet of small labels and checks the number again. The new label needs to match the record rather than simply resemble the old one. A short note identifies which shelf should receive the book. He then examines the remaining stack, looking for loose pages and damaged bindings. Through the open doorway he can see a visitor replacing a magazine on the reading table. He moves an empty tray closer and begins checking the next group of returned books.'''),
('laundry','''Water leaves damp cloth through evaporation, but the rate depends on more than the amount of water initially present. Spreading a towel exposes more of its surface to the surrounding air than leaving it folded in a thick heap. Air can move around the exposed fibers and carry away some of the water vapor. If the nearby air is already very humid, drying may be slower. A towel hanging outside can dry differently from one left in a small enclosed room, even when the temperature feels similar.
The arrangement of the cloth also affects how water collects. A heavy fold can hold moisture close to other wet layers, while a single hanging layer gives the air more access. Gravity may move liquid water toward a lower edge before it evaporates. Different fabrics absorb and release water differently because their fibers and construction are not identical. A thin shirt and a thick cotton towel therefore need not dry at the same speed. Looking only at their outer surfaces can hide moisture remaining deeper inside.
Someone arranging laundry on a line leaves gaps between larger items. The gaps let moving air pass through instead of forcing every piece of cloth against its neighbor. The person checks that pegs hold the fabric securely without stretching a delicate edge. A sleeve caught inside a shirt may remain damp after the rest feels dry. Turning or rearranging an item can expose that part. The line itself is positioned so the cloth does not drag against a dusty surface. Nearby branches and walls can alter how much air reaches it.
Indoors, drying clothes adds water vapor to the room. Suitable ventilation helps remove that moisture rather than allowing it to accumulate around the fabric. The person checks the conditions instead of assuming that stronger heat is always the best approach. Some materials need particular care and should be handled according to their labels. A small rack can be arranged to keep thicker items from overlapping. The person feels along a seam where several layers meet, compares it with the open center of the fabric, and moves the next garment to give both areas more space.'''),
('map','''A paper map uses symbols to represent features that would be much larger in the landscape. A line may show a road, a path or a stream, depending on the legend. Looking at that legend before planning a route helps prevent a familiar-looking mark from being misunderstood. The map's scale describes how a distance on the page relates to distance on the ground. A short straight measurement may not capture every bend in a winding path. The person using the map considers the shape of the route as well as the positions of its endpoints.
Near a trail entrance, a walker places the map on a flat surface and identifies two nearby landmarks. A bridge and a junction are easier to recognize than an isolated patch of trees. The walker turns the map so that its arrangement corresponds more closely to the surroundings. A compass can help, but its use also depends on understanding which direction the map indicates. The walker checks the destination and chooses a route appropriate to the available time. A path marked on paper is not automatically suitable for every person or every weather condition.
As the walk continues, clear landmarks provide opportunities to compare the map with the ground. The walker notes the next junction before reaching it rather than relying on memory of an earlier turn. A bend in a stream appears beside the path, followed by a low stone wall. These features help confirm the location. If the surroundings stop matching the expected route, continuing without checking can increase the uncertainty. The walker can pause, look back at the last known landmark, and reconsider the interpretation of the map.
At a small crossing, the map shows two paths separated by only a narrow line. On the ground, one path climbs while the other stays near the water. The walker examines the contour markings and notices how closely they are spaced beside the climbing route. An older sign points toward a village, but some of its lettering has faded. The walker compares the sign with the printed names on the map and looks for the bridge marked a little farther along the lower path. A companion holds the edge of the paper steady while they inspect that part more closely.'''),
('echo','''Sound travels through air as a disturbance that can move away from its source. When it reaches a large surface, some of it may be reflected. If the reflected sound reaches a listener sufficiently later than the original, it can be heard as a separate echo. The delay depends in part on how far the sound travels to the surface and back. A nearby wall may produce reflections that blend into the original sound instead. A distant cliff can make a more distinct repetition possible under suitable conditions.
The nature of the surface matters as well. A hard, broad surface can reflect sound differently from a soft, irregular one. Curtains and upholstered furniture often absorb more sound than bare walls and floors. A room can contain many reflections arriving from different directions. These can overlap and make speech less clear, even when no separate echo is obvious. Moving a speaker or listener can change the mixture of direct and reflected sound. The size and shape of the room influence the routes available to those reflections.
In an empty hall, a person claps once and listens. The sound seems to remain in the room for a short time after the hands stop moving. Another person walks toward a side wall and repeats the clap from there. The result is not identical because the distances to the surrounding surfaces have changed. They avoid making several sounds at once, which would make the comparison harder. A notebook records where each person stood and what they noticed. Their descriptions distinguish a clear repeated sound from a less distinct continuation.
They then look at the materials around the hall. Some windows have curtains drawn across them, while another section of wall remains exposed. A stack of folded fabric sits on a low platform. The people discuss how changing the arrangement might affect later observations, but they keep the current test conditions unchanged while comparing positions. One person stands near the middle of the room and speaks a short sentence at an ordinary volume. The listener moves a few steps to the side, paying attention to how easily the words can be understood from that new position.'''),
('bread','''Bread dough usually begins as a mixture of flour, water and other ingredients chosen for a particular recipe. When yeast is present, it can consume available sugars and release carbon dioxide. Small gas bubbles form within the dough. Proteins from the flour help produce a flexible network that can hold some of those bubbles. As the gas accumulates, the dough can expand. Its behavior depends on the ingredients, the way it has been mixed and the conditions in which it rests. A warmer place can speed some processes, but excessive heat can damage the yeast.
A baker checks the dough by looking at its surface and handling it gently. A smooth appearance does not reveal everything about the inside. The dough may feel elastic, sticky or firm, and those impressions can change as it rests. The baker compares it with an earlier batch made from the same recipe. Even small differences in the amount of water can affect how readily it stretches. Flour also varies, so the written quantities provide a starting point rather than an explanation of every detail. The baker keeps notes about the mixture and the room conditions.
During shaping, the baker works on a lightly prepared surface and tries not to tear the dough unnecessarily. The aim is to arrange it for the next stage while retaining a useful structure. A bowl or cloth can help support a soft piece, depending on the method being used. The dough then rests again. Its surface gradually changes as gas develops inside. Watching this process is more informative than relying only on a clock. Two pieces of different sizes may also respond differently, even when they came from the same mixture.
Inside an oven, heat changes both the dough and the gas it contains. The loaf eventually becomes firm enough to preserve many of the spaces formed earlier. The crust and the interior do not heat in precisely the same way. A baker uses the method appropriate to the recipe to judge progress rather than opening the oven repeatedly without a reason. On a nearby tray, another shaped piece is still resting. The baker compares its outline with the mark left on a cloth earlier and checks whether the surface has begun to dry before preparing it for the next step.'''),
('market_story','''The market stalls were beginning to open when Daniel reached the square. A fruit seller was arranging apples in shallow wooden boxes, turning the bruised sides away from the edge where they might be pressed by another box. Beside the apples stood a small handwritten sign showing the varieties available that morning. Daniel stopped to look at a row of pears and asked which ones would be ready to eat later in the week. The seller picked up two pears and explained the difference she noticed when holding them gently.
At the next stall, a baker placed wrapped loaves on a cloth-covered table. A customer asked whether a smaller loaf could be cut in half. The baker checked which loaves were available and brought a clean board closer. Behind him, someone folded empty paper bags and arranged them beside a basket. Daniel moved aside to let another customer reach the counter. The smell of bread mixed with the scent of herbs from a nearby display. A light breeze lifted one corner of the cloth, and the baker put a small weight on it.
Farther across the square, a woman was unpacking bunches of flowers from a bucket. She trimmed a few damaged stems and placed the bunches where passersby could see their colors. A child beside her sorted short pieces of string into a small tin. They spoke about which bucket would fit beneath the table when the display was complete. Daniel paused to examine a bunch of yellow flowers, then looked toward the stall where his sister had agreed to meet him. She was standing beside a stack of empty baskets and reading a sign.
They walked together along the shaded side of the square, discussing what they might prepare for supper. His sister carried a folded shopping list, but she changed her plan when they saw the vegetables on one stall. The seller described which produce had arrived that morning and showed them a smaller basket kept beneath the table. Daniel compared two bunches while his sister asked about their condition. Nearby, someone was moving a trolley through a gap between stalls. They stepped closer to the table to leave enough room and continued looking over the vegetables.'''),
('river','''A narrow river moves through a stretch of countryside where the banks change from grass to exposed stone. Near one bank, a few leaves turn in a small circle rather than moving directly downstream. A submerged stone changes the direction of the current and creates a quieter patch behind it. An observer watches a leaf pass the edge of that patch and enter faster water again. Reflections from the sky make some movements difficult to see, so the observer changes position and looks across the surface from a lower angle.
Farther along the bank, the river bends around a low ridge. Water does not move at the same speed across the whole bend. The shape of the channel and the condition of the bottom both influence its path. Fine material can collect in a quieter area, while another part remains more exposed. A small footbridge provides a view of these differences from above. The observer stands near its middle and follows a line of ripples toward the far bank. A breeze changes the reflection of nearby trees without stopping the movement underneath.
A worker is checking marked poles at several points along the water. The marks allow a comparison of the water level from one visit to another. A single measurement cannot describe every change along the river, so the worker notes the location as well as the reading. Recent rain farther upstream may affect the level even if the immediate surroundings have remained dry. The observer asks how the poles were placed and looks at a sketch showing their relative positions. The worker points toward another marker partly hidden by reeds.
They walk along a path that rises slightly above the bank. Grass grows between stones near the edge, and a small side channel enters beneath an overhanging branch. The worker examines that junction before taking another reading. A thin line of floating material shows where two movements of water meet. The observer draws the outline of the junction in a notebook and adds an arrow indicating the visible flow. From this position, the bridge appears at an angle behind the reeds. The worker moves toward the next pole while the observer looks more closely at the water passing the stones.''')]
